{{Nihongo|'''Kyūshū'''|{{linktext|九州}}|Kyūshū|{{IPA|ja|kʲɯꜜːɕɯː|pron|ja-Kyushu.ogg}}, {{lit}} 'Nine {{ill|Provinces of Japan|en|Provinces of Japan|qid=Q860290|lt=Provinces|1=Provinces of Japan}}'}} is the third-largest island of {{ill|Japan|en|Japan|qid=Q17|lt=Japan|1=Japan}}'s [[:en:Japanese Archipelago|four main islands]] and the most southerly of the four largest islands (i.e. excluding {{ill|Okinawa Island|en|Okinawa Island|qid=Q600614|lt=Okinawa|1=Okinawa Island}}).<ref name="main-islands-japan">{{cite web |title=離島とは(島の基礎知識) (what is a remote island?) |publisher=[[Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism]] |url=http://www.mlit.go.jp/crd/chirit/ritoutoha.html |website=MLIT (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism) |date=22 August 2015 |access-date=9 August 2019 |language=ja |format=website |quote=MILT classification 6,852 islands(main islands: 5 islands, remote islands: 6,847 islands) |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071113053915/http://www.mlit.go.jp/crd/chirit/ritoutoha.html |archive-date=2007-11-13}}</ref><ref>Nussbaum, Louis-Frédéric. (2005). "Kyūshū" in {{Google books|p2QnPijAEmEC|''Japan Encyclopedia'', p. 588|page=588}}</ref> In the past, it has been known as {{nihongo3|"Nine Countries"|九国|Kyūkoku}}, {{nihongo3|"West of the Pacified Area"|鎮西|Chinzei}} and {{nihongo3|"Island of Tsukushi"|{{lang|ja|筑紫島}}|Tsukushi-no-shima}}. The historical regional name {{nihongo|{{ill|Saikaidō|en|Saikaidō|qid=Q1334003|lt=Saikaidō|1=Saikaidō}}|{{lang|ja|西海道}}||{{lit}} West Sea [[:en:Circuit (country subdivision)|Circuit]]}} referred to Kyushu and its surrounding islands. Kyushu has a land area of {{convert|36782|km2|sqmi}} and a population of 14,311,224 in 2018.<ref name="thoughtco"/>

In the 8th-century {{ill|Taihō Code|en|Taihō Code|qid=Q1059892|lt=Taihō Code|1=Taihō Code}} reforms, {{ill|Dazaifu (government)|en|Dazaifu (government)|qid=Q144845|lt=Dazaifu|1=Dazaifu (government)}} was established as a special administrative term for the region.<ref>Nussbaum, "Dazaifu" in {{Google books|p2QnPijAEmEC|p. 150|page=150}}; [http://www.japan-guide.com/e/e4850.html Dazaifu]</ref>

==Geography==
[[File:Kyushu-Japan-ISS-Space.png|thumb|250px|Kyushu from the {{ill|International Space Station|en|International Space Station|qid=Q25271|lt=International Space Station|1=International Space Station}}]]
[[File:Geofeatures_map_of_Kyushu_Japan_ja.svg|thumb|315x315px|Geofeatures map of Kyushu]]

The island is mountainous, and Japan's most active volcano, {{ill|Mount Aso|en|Mount Aso|qid=Q733710|lt=Mount Aso|1=Mount Aso}} at {{convert|1591|m}}, is on Kyushu. There are many other signs of tectonic activity, including numerous areas of {{ill|Hot spring|en|Hot spring|qid=Q177380|lt=Hot spring|1=Hot spring}}s. The most famous of these are in [[:en:Beppu, Ōita|Beppu]], on the east shore, and around Mt. Aso in central Kyushu. The island is separated from [[Honshu]] by the {{ill|Kanmon Straits|en|Kanmon Straits|qid=Q494894|lt=Kanmon Straits|1=Kanmon Straits}}. Being the nearest island to the {{ill|Asia|en|Asia|qid=Q48|lt=Asian continent|1=Asia}},<ref>Nussbaum, Louis-Frédéric and Käthe Roth (2005). [https://books.google.com/books?id=p2QnPijAEmEC ''Japan Encyclopedia'']. Cambridge, Mass.: Harvard University Press. {{ISBN|978-0-674-01753-5}}. {{OCLC|58053128}}.
</ref> historically it is the gateway to Japan.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Kyushu, gateway to Japan : a concise history |first=Andrew |last=Cobbing |date=2009 |publisher=Global Oriental |isbn=9789004213128 |pages=157 |oclc=754792858}}</ref>

The total area is {{convert|36,782.37|km²|sqmi|abbr=on}} which makes it the 37th {{ill|en:List of islands by area|en|en:List of islands by area|qid=Q104176|lt=largest island|1=List of islands by area}} in the world.<ref name="island1">{{Cite web |date=2016-10-01 |url=http://www.gsi.go.jp/KOKUJYOHO/MENCHO/201610/f3_shima.pdf |title=平成28th year 全国都道府県市区町村別面積調 島面積 |publisher=[[国土地理院]] |accessdate=2017-02-27}}</ref> It's slightly larger than {{ill|Geography of Taiwan|en|Geography of Taiwan|qid=Q22528|lt=Taiwan|1=Geography of Taiwan}} island {{convert|35,808|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}.<ref name="thoughtco">{{Cite news|url=https://www.thoughtco.com/islands-of-japan-1435071|title=Discover the Geography of the 4 Main Islands of Japan |work=ThoughtCo |access-date=2018-09-26}}</ref> The highest elevation is {{convert|1791|m|0|abbr=off|comma=5|sp=us}} on [[Mount Kujū]].<ref name="peaklist">{{cite web |url=http://www.peaklist.org/WWlists/ultras/japan.html |title=Japan Ultra-Prominences |publisher=Peaklist.org |accessdate=2015-01-01}}</ref>

The name ''Kyūshū'' comes from the nine ancient provinces of {{ill|Saikaidō|en|Saikaidō|qid=Q1334003|lt=Saikaidō|1=Saikaidō}} situated on the island: {{ill|Chikuzen Province|en|Chikuzen Province|qid=Q1047664|lt=Chikuzen|1=Chikuzen Province}}, {{ill|Chikugo Province|en|Chikugo Province|qid=Q864159|lt=Chikugo|1=Chikugo Province}}, {{ill|Hizen Province|en|Hizen Province|qid=Q906926|lt=Hizen|1=Hizen Province}}, {{ill|Higo Province|en|Higo Province|qid=Q259705|lt=Higo|1=Higo Province}}, {{ill|Buzen Province|en|Buzen Province|qid=Q1047134|lt=Buzen|1=Buzen Province}}, {{ill|Bungo Province|en|Bungo Province|qid=Q864148|lt=Bungo|1=Bungo Province}}, {{ill|Hyūga Province|en|Hyūga Province|qid=Q513737|lt=Hyūga|1=Hyūga Province}}, {{ill|Ōsumi Province|en|Ōsumi Province|qid=Q981002|lt=Osumi|1=Ōsumi Province}}, and {{ill|Satsuma Province|en|Satsuma Province|qid=Q667475|lt=Satsuma|1=Satsuma Province}}.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Kyushu, Gateway to Japan: A Concise History |first=Andrew |last=Cobbing |date=2009 |publisher=Global Oriental |isbn=9789004213128 |pages=3 |oclc=754792858}}</ref>

Today's {{nihongo|Kyushu Region|{{lang|ja|九州地方}}|Kyūshū-chihō}} is a politically defined region that consists of the seven {{ill|Prefectures of Japan|en|Prefectures of Japan|qid=Q50337|lt=prefecture|1=Prefectures of Japan}}s on the island of Kyushu (which also includes the former {{ill|Tsushima Province|en|Tsushima Province|qid=Q1054861|lt=Tsushima|1=Tsushima Province}} and {{ill|Iki Province|en|Iki Province|qid=Q1052938|lt=Iki|1=Iki Province}} as part of Nagasaki), plus {{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa Prefecture|1=Okinawa Prefecture}} to the south:
* {{ill|Northern Kyushu|en|Northern Kyushu|qid=Q397703|lt=Northern Kyushu|1=Northern Kyushu}}
** {{ill|Fukuoka Prefecture|en|Fukuoka Prefecture|qid=Q123258|lt=Fukuoka Prefecture|1=Fukuoka Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Kumamoto Prefecture|en|Kumamoto Prefecture|qid=Q130308|lt=Kumamoto Prefecture|1=Kumamoto Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Nagasaki Prefecture|en|Nagasaki Prefecture|qid=Q169376|lt=Nagasaki Prefecture|1=Nagasaki Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Ōita Prefecture|en|Ōita Prefecture|qid=Q133924|lt=Ōita Prefecture|1=Ōita Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Saga Prefecture|en|Saga Prefecture|qid=Q160420|lt=Saga Prefecture|1=Saga Prefecture}}
* {{ill|Southern Kyushu|en|Southern Kyushu|qid=Q8017707|lt=Southern Kyushu|1=Southern Kyushu}}
** {{ill|Kagoshima Prefecture|en|Kagoshima Prefecture|qid=Q15701|lt=Kagoshima Prefecture|1=Kagoshima Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Miyazaki Prefecture|en|Miyazaki Prefecture|qid=Q130300|lt=Miyazaki Prefecture|1=Miyazaki Prefecture}}
*{{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa|1=Okinawa Prefecture}}
** {{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa Prefecture|1=Okinawa Prefecture}}

==Population==

Kyushu has 10.3{{nbsp}}percent of the population of Japan.<ref name=honshu2>{{cite book| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=90C4DgAAQBAJ&pg=PA16| page=16| title=The Philippine Archipelago |first=Yves |last=Boquet |publisher=Springer |year= 2017|isbn= 9783319519265}}</ref> Most of Kyushu's population is concentrated along the northwest, in the cities of {{ill|Fukuoka|en|Fukuoka|qid=Q26600|lt=Fukuoka|1=Fukuoka}} and {{ill|Kitakyushu|en|Kitakyushu|qid=Q188806|lt=Kitakyushu|1=Kitakyushu}}, with population corridors stretching southwest into {{ill|Sasebo|en|Sasebo|qid=Q328000|lt=Sasebo|1=Sasebo}} and {{ill|Nagasaki|en|Nagasaki|qid=Q38234|lt=Nagasaki|1=Nagasaki}} and south into {{ill|Kumamoto|en|Kumamoto|qid=Q199889|lt=Kumamoto|1=Kumamoto}} and {{ill|Kagoshima|en|Kagoshima|qid=Q15674|lt=Kagoshima|1=Kagoshima}}. Except for {{ill|Ōita (city)|en|Ōita (city)|qid=Q199609|lt=Oita|1=Ōita (city)}} and {{ill|Miyazaki (city)|en|Miyazaki (city)|qid=Q204308|lt=Miyazaki|1=Miyazaki (city)}}, the eastern seaboard shows a general {{ill|Demographics of Japan|en|Demographics of Japan|qid=Q1124220|lt=decline in population|1=Demographics of Japan}}.

Politically, Kyushu is described as a stronghold of the {{ill|Liberal Democratic Party (Japan)|en|Liberal Democratic Party (Japan)|qid=Q232595|lt=Liberal Democratic Party|1=Liberal Democratic Party (Japan)}}.<ref name="veconomist" >{{cite news|title=Japanese voters want a plan to handle a declining population|url=https://www.economist.com/news/asia/21730003-election-campaign-disappointing-them-japanese-voters-want-plan-handle-declining|newspaper={{ill|The Economist|en|The Economist|qid=Q180089|lt=The Economist|1=The Economist}}|date=5 October 2017}}</ref>

Per Japanese census data,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.citypopulation.de/en/japan/cities/fukuoka/|title=Fukuoka (Japan): Prefecture, Major Cities & Towns - Population Statistics, Maps, Charts, Weather and Web Information |website=www.citypopulation.de}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.demographia.com/db-japanpref.htm|title=Japan Prefectures Population from 1920 and Area |website=www.demographia.com}}</ref> the Kyushu region's population with {{ill|Ryukyu Islands|en|Ryukyu Islands|qid=Q6897416|lt=Ryukyu Islands|1=Ryukyu Islands}} ({{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa|1=Okinawa Prefecture}} and {{ill|Kagoshima Prefecture|en|Kagoshima Prefecture|qid=Q15701|lt=Kagoshima|1=Kagoshima Prefecture}} Prefectures) has experienced a large population decline since around 2000. However, the population decline in total is mild because of the relatively high birth rate of {{ill|Ryukyuans|en|Ryukyuans|qid=Q1208167|lt=Ryukyuans|1=Ryukyuans}} both within the Ryukyuan lands ({{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa|1=Okinawa Prefecture}} and {{ill|Kagoshima Prefecture|en|Kagoshima Prefecture|qid=Q15701|lt=Kagoshima|1=Kagoshima Prefecture}}) and throughout the Kyushu region. In addition, the other prefectures in Kyushu also have exceptionally high [[Total fertility rate#Japan|TFR]]s compared to the rest of Japan.<ref>[https://www8.cao.go.jp/shoushi/shoushika/research/cyousa23/kankyo/pdf/5.pdf 「都市と地方における子育て環境の違いから得られた知見」]</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mhlw.go.jp/toukei/saikin/hw/jinkou/geppo/nengai20/dl/h9.pdf|title=令和２年(2020)人口動態統計月報年計（概数）の概況}}</ref> The Ryukyuans are an {{ill|Indigenous peoples|en|Indigenous peoples|qid=Q103817|lt=indigenous|1=Indigenous peoples}} minority group in Japan.

