==Environment and agriculture==
Parts of Kyushu have a [[:en:Subtropical climate|Subtropical climate]], particularly {{ill|Miyazaki Prefecture|en|Miyazaki Prefecture|qid=Q130300|lt=Miyazaki Prefecture|1=Miyazaki Prefecture}} and {{ill|Kagoshima Prefecture|en|Kagoshima Prefecture|qid=Q15701|lt=Kagoshima Prefecture|1=Kagoshima Prefecture}}. Major agricultural products are {{ill|Rice|en|Rice|qid=Q5090|lt=Rice|1=Rice}}, {{ill|Tea|en|Tea|qid=Q6097|lt=Tea|1=Tea}}, {{ill|Tobacco|en|Tobacco|qid=Q1566|lt=Tobacco|1=Tobacco}}, {{ill|Sweet potato|en|Sweet potato|qid=Q37937|lt=Sweet potato|1=Sweet potato}}es, and [[:en:Soy|Soy]]; also, {{ill|Silk|en|Silk|qid=Q37681|lt=Silk|1=Silk}} is widely produced.

Besides the [[:en:Volcanic|Volcanic]] area of the south, there are significant mud {{ill|Hot spring|en|Hot spring|qid=Q177380|lt=Hot spring|1=Hot spring}} in the northern part of the island, around {{ill|Beppu|en|Beppu|qid=Q273880|lt=Beppu|1=Beppu}}. The springs are the site of occurrence of certain {{ill|Extremophile|en|Extremophile|qid=Q206912|lt=Extremophile|1=Extremophile}} {{ill|Microorganism|en|Microorganism|qid=Q39833|lt=Microorganism|1=Microorganism}}s, which are capable of surviving in extremely hot environments.<ref>C. Michael Hogan. 2010. [http://www.eoearth.org/article/Extremophile?topic=49540 ''Extremophile'']. eds. E. Monosson and C. Cleveland. Encyclopedia of Earth. National Council for Science and the Environment, Washington DC</ref>

There are two World Natural Heritage sites in Kyushu: {{ill|Yakushima|en|Yakushima|qid=Q242176|lt=Yakushima|1=Yakushima}} (registered in 1993) and {{ill|Amami-Ōshima Island, Tokunoshima Island, northern part of Okinawa Island, and Iriomote Island|en|Amami-Ōshima Island, Tokunoshima Island, northern part of Okinawa Island, and Iriomote Island|qid=Q11444847|lt=Amami-Ōshima Island, Tokunoshima Island, northern part of Okinawa Island, and Iriomote Island|1=Amami-Ōshima Island, Tokunoshima Island, northern part of Okinawa Island, and Iriomote Island}} (registered in 2021).<ref name="heritage">{{Cite web|title=日本の世界遺産一覧 |url=https://www.bunka.go.jp/seisaku/bunkazai/shokai/sekai_isan/ichiran/|website=文化庁|language=ja| access-date=2023-05-22}}</ref>

==Economy ==
{{main|Economy of Japan}}
[[Image:M-V with ASTRO-E2 on launch pad.jpeg|thumb|M-V rocket launch rehearsal at USC]]
Kyushu's economy accounts for about 10% of Japan's total, and with a GDP equivalent to that of Iran, the 26th largest country in the world, it is the fourth largest economic zone after the three major metropolitan areas of {{ill|Tokyo|en|Tokyo|qid=Q1490|lt=Tokyo|1=Tokyo}}, {{ill|Osaka|en|Osaka|qid=Q35765|lt=Osaka|1=Osaka}}, and {{ill|Nagoya|en|Nagoya|qid=Q11751|lt=Nagoya|1=Nagoya}}.<ref name="FFG">{{Cite web|title=potential of Kyushu |url=https://www.fukuoka-fg.com/vision/03.html|website=Fukuoka financial group|language=ja|access-date=2023-05-16}}</ref>

Kyushu's economy has a well-balanced industrial structure, ranging from primary industries such as agriculture, to secondary industries such as manufacturing, and tertiary industries such as retail, services, and tourism. Agricultural output in the region amounts to 1.8 trillion yen (20% share of the national total), and the region is a major domestic production center for the [[:en:Automobile|Automobile]] and {{ill|Semiconductor|en|Semiconductor|qid=Q11456|lt=Semiconductor|1=Semiconductor}} industries. Kyushu also has a thriving healthcare industry, including medical and nursing care, and numerous research and manufacturing facilities in the fields of {{ill|Hydrogen|en|Hydrogen|qid=Q556|lt=Hydrogen|1=Hydrogen}}, {{ill|Solar power|en|Solar power|qid=Q1483757|lt=Solar power|1=Solar power}}, and other renewable energies. Furthermore, [[:en:Fukuoka City|Fukuoka City]], [[:en:Kitakyushu City|Kitakyushu City]] and {{ill|Okinawa Prefecture|en|Okinawa Prefecture|qid=Q766445|lt=Okinawa Prefecture|1=Okinawa Prefecture}} have been designated as [[National Strategic Special Zones]], which are expected to have an economic ripple effect on the entire Kyushu region through the creation of innovation in industry and the promotion of new entrepreneurship and start-ups.<ref name="FFG" />

Kyushu is a region with strong economic ties to Asia. For example, Asia accounted for 420 (77.9%) of the 539 overseas expansion cases of Kyushu-Yamaguchi companies from 2010 to 2019, and Asia accounted for 61.1% of Kyushu-Yamaguchi's total exports in 2019, 7.4 percentage points higher than the nation as a whole. As the logistics node between Japan and Asia, the ports of Hakata and Kitakyushu handle a large number of international containers. In addition, the number of cruise ship calls in 2019 was 772, with Kyushu accounting for 26.9% of the nation's total.<ref>{{Cite web|title=はじめての九州経済 |url=https://www.kerc.or.jp/economy/gaikan/|website=Kyushu economic research center|language=ja|access-date=2023-05-22}}</ref>

Kyushu  is noted for various types of {{ill|Porcelain|en|Porcelain|qid=Q130693|lt=Porcelain|1=Porcelain}}, including Arita, Imari, Satsuma, and Karatsu. {{ill|Heavy industry|en|Heavy industry|qid=Q622662|lt=Heavy industry|1=Heavy industry}} is concentrated in the north around Fukuoka, Kitakyushu, Nagasaki, and Oita and includes chemicals, automobiles, semiconductors, metal processing, shipbuilding, etc.<ref>{{Cite web|title=JAPAN PRODUCTS: Business Directory of Japanese Companies.|url=http://japan-product.com/|language=en-US|access-date=2020-05-28}}</ref> The island of {{ill|Tanegashima|en|Tanegashima|qid=Q696228|lt=Tanegashima|1=Tanegashima}} hosts the {{ill|Tanegashima Space Center|en|Tanegashima Space Center|qid=Q742683|lt=Tanegashima Space Center|1=Tanegashima Space Center}}, which is the largest rocket-launch complex in Japan.

==Transportation==
Kyushu is linked to the larger island of [[Honshu]] by the {{ill|Kanmon Railway Tunnel|en|Kanmon Railway Tunnel|qid=Q15972411|lt=Kanmon Railway Tunnel|1=Kanmon Railway Tunnel}},<ref>{{Cite web|title=Kanmon Tunnel {{!}} Description, History, Construction, & Facts |url=https://www.britannica.com/topic/Kanmon-Tunnel|access-date=2021-10-20|website=Encyclopedia Britannica |language=en}}</ref> which carries the non-{{ill|Shinkansen|en|Shinkansen|qid=Q164338|lt=Shinkansen|1=Shinkansen}} trains of the {{ill|Kyushu Railway Company|en|Kyushu Railway Company|qid=Q498366|lt=Kyushu Railway Company|1=Kyushu Railway Company}}, and the newer {{ill|Shin-Kanmon Tunnel|en|Shin-Kanmon Tunnel|qid=Q8016189|lt=Shin-Kanmon Tunnel|1=Shin-Kanmon Tunnel}} carrying the {{ill|San'yō Shinkansen|en|San'yō Shinkansen|qid=Q615964|lt=San'yō Shinkansen|1=San'yō Shinkansen}}. Railways on the island are operated by the Kyushu Railway Company and {{ill|West Japan Railway Company|en|West Japan Railway Company|qid=Q502125|lt=West Japan Railway Company|1=West Japan Railway Company}}, as well as a variety of smaller companies such as {{ill|Amagi Railway Amagi Line|en|Amagi Railway Amagi Line|qid=Q642407|lt=Amagi Railway|1=Amagi Railway Amagi Line}}<ref>{{Cite web|title=Railways in Kyushu|url=https://www.sinfin.net/railways/world/japan/kyushu.html|access-date=2021-10-20|website=www.sinfin.net}}</ref> and {{ill|Nishi-Nippon Railroad|en|Nishi-Nippon Railroad|qid=Q869251|lt=Nishitetsu Railway|1=Nishi-Nippon Railroad}}.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Railways in Northern Kyushu|url=http://www.japaneserailwaysociety.com/jrs/members/oliver/kyushu/kyushu.htm|access-date=2021-10-20|website=www.japaneserailwaysociety.com}}</ref> {{ill|Kyushu Shinkansen|en|Kyushu Shinkansen|qid=Q11370254|lt=Kyushu Shinkansen|1=Kyushu Shinkansen}} trains operate between major cities on the island,<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Getting Around {{!}} Kyushu Tourism Promotion Organization|url=https://www.visit-kyushu.com/en/plan-your-trip/getting-around/|access-date=2021-10-20|website=www.visit-kyushu.com|language=en}}</ref> such as {{ill|Fukuoka|en|Fukuoka|qid=Q26600|lt=Fukuoka|1=Fukuoka}} and {{ill|Kagoshima|en|Kagoshima|qid=Q15674|lt=Kagoshima|1=Kagoshima}},<ref>{{Cite web|title=Kyushu Shinkansen|url=https://www.japanvisitor.com/japan-travel/kyushu-shinkansen|url-status=live|access-date=2021-10-20|website=[[Japan Visitor]] |archive-date=2021-10-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020112050/https://www.japanvisitor.com/japan-travel/kyushu-shinkansen}}</ref> with an additional route between {{STN|Takeo-Onsen}} and {{ill|Nagasaki|en|Nagasaki|qid=Q38234|lt=Nagasaki|1=Nagasaki}} which is in operation since September 2022.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-07-10|title=Fukuoka to Nagasaki: How to go from Fukuoka to Nagasaki? {{!}} Japan-Rail-Pass|url=https://www.japan-rail-pass.com/node/4326|access-date=2022-04-21|website=Japan Rail Pass {{!}} Japan Rail Pass |language=en}}</ref> Kyushu is also known for its scenic train services, such as the Limited Express Yufuin no Mori and Limited Express Kawasemi Yamasemi.<ref name=":0" />

The {{ill|Kanmon Bridge|en|Kanmon Bridge|qid=Q1196470|lt=Kanmon Bridge|1=Kanmon Bridge}} and {{ill|Kanmon Roadway Tunnel|en|Kanmon Roadway Tunnel|qid=Q15972446|lt=Kanmon Roadway Tunnel|1=Kanmon Roadway Tunnel}} also connect the island with Honshu, allowing for vehicular transport between the two.<ref>{{Cite web|last=MATCHA |title=Walk From Honshu To Kyushu! 4 Ways To Cross Between The Two Islands|url=https://matcha-jp.com/en/1737|access-date=2021-10-20|website=MATCHA - JAPAN TRAVEL WEB MAGAZINE |language=en}}</ref> The {{ill|Kyushu Expressway|en|Kyushu Expressway|qid=Q1060803|lt=Kyushu Expressway|1=Kyushu Expressway}} spans the length of the island, linking the {{ill|Higashikyushu Expressway|en|Higashikyushu Expressway|qid=Q2648028|lt=Higashikyushu Expressway|1=Higashikyushu Expressway}} and Ibusuki Skyline, connecting major cities such as {{ill|Fukuoka|en|Fukuoka|qid=Q26600|lt=Fukuoka|1=Fukuoka}} and {{ill|Kumamoto|en|Kumamoto|qid=Q199889|lt=Kumamoto|1=Kumamoto}} along the way.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kyushu Expy · Japan |url=https://www.google.com/maps/place/Kyushu+Expy,+Japan/@32.7319959,130.7735981,17z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m5!3m4!1s0x3540f3427949e6d9:0x60891c7c25263c3!8m2!3d32.7319914!4d130.7757868 |access-date=2022-11-18 |website=Kyushu Expy · Japan |language=en}}</ref> There are also many quiet country roads, including popular tourist routes such as the {{ill|Nichinan, Miyazaki|en|Nichinan, Miyazaki|qid=Q860669|lt=Nichinan|1=Nichinan, Miyazaki}} coast road and the Aso Panorama Line in {{ill|Kumamoto Prefecture|en|Kumamoto Prefecture|qid=Q130308|lt=Kumamoto Prefecture|1=Kumamoto Prefecture}}. Bus services are available and cover 2,400 routes within Kyushu's cities, connecting many other destinations.<ref name=":0" />

Several passenger and car ferry services connect both northern and southern Kyushu with main port cities on the main island of Honshu (Kobe, Osaka, Tokyo) and [[Shikoku]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.japan-guide.com/e/e2355.html|title = Domestic Ferries| date=May 2022 }}</ref>

